/**
 * Problem spoke — "something is scratching in my attic at night."
 *
 * THE ARGUMENT: time of day narrows the list, then droppings, hole size and an
 * inspection settle it. Sound alone does not identify an animal, and the page
 * says so on the authority of the wildlife damage literature itself.
 *
 * VERIFIED:
 *  - ICWDM, verbatim and load-bearing: "Noises and sounds in wildlife damage
 *    settings are not a very accurate way to determine the species causing a
 *    problem." Its own noise table: scratching in wall → rats, mice, birds;
 *    running in attic → squirrels; heavy walking → raccoon or opossum.
 *  - UC IPM 74122: "Tree squirrels are active during the day"; most active
 *    early morning and late afternoon; one or two litters a year. Newspaper
 *    plug test, verbatim: "One way to test whether any squirrels are remaining
 *    is to plug the entrance with a loose wad of newspaper; if any remain
 *    inside they will remove the plug to get out."
 *  - UC IPM 74106: "Rats are mostly active at night." Roof rats nest in
 *    attics, wall voids, ceiling voids and cabinets.
 *  - MU G9442: "Mice occasionally are seen during daylight hours, but they are
 *    most active at night."; "House mice have a characteristic musky odor";
 *    traps no more than about 10 feet apart.
 *  - ICWDM raccoons: "Raccoons are nocturnal."; "Most litters are born in
 *    April or May" with late litters into June–August; 3 to 5 young.
 *  - ICWDM bats: droppings "segmented, elongated, and friable", powdery when
 *    crushed with "shiny bits of undigested insect remains"; rodent droppings
 *    "harder, and are more fibrous". Activity peaks "on hot days in attics,
 *    before leaving the roost at dusk, and upon returning at dawn."
 *  - ICWDM hole sizes: fox and gray squirrels "the size of a baseball"; red
 *    squirrels "the size of a golf ball"; flying squirrels "the size of a
 *    quarter".
 *  - AgriLife Insects in the City, "Rodents in attics": roof rats enter
 *    "through a gap under a door as narrow as 1/2 inch, or a hole the size of
 *    a quarter"; entry points "gaps around the garage door, venting and
 *    flashing on the roof, and ground-level gaps under and around siding";
 *    squirrel damage "sometimes opening large holes in soffits and fascia, and
 *    chewing on wires"; one-way door or repeater trap on the roof.
 *  - UGA B1248: "Attic noise during the day could be gray squirrels."; fresh
 *    droppings "black, shiny, and moist. Old droppings are dry and brown or
 *    gray."; order of control: habitat modification → exclusion → removal →
 *    lethal. Sparrows, starlings and pigeons unprotected; nearly all other
 *    native birds protected under the MBTA.
 *
 * ⚠️ LEGAL SECTION — THE PART A PEST SITE MOST OFTEN GETS WRONG:
 *  - BATS: the citation is TEXAS PARKS AND WILDLIFE CODE § 63.101, NOT the
 *    Health & Safety Code. § 63.101(a) bars hunting, sale, purchase or
 *    possession-after-purchase; (b) permits removal or hunting of bats inside
 *    or on buildings occupied by people; (c) exempts animal control officers,
 *    peace officers, health officials, persons transporting a bat for rabies
 *    testing, and licensed pest control service providers. AgriLife: "It is a
 *    federal violation to use chemicals—including insecticides, rodenticides,
 *    disinfectants, and mothballs—to kill bats."
 *  - BAT TIMING: AgriLife's Texas window is "between September and April,
 *    depending on your location in the state"; "Evicting the adults during
 *    maternity season will orphan and kill the babies inside the building."
 *    One-way devices stay "at least 1 week during warm weather and 2 weeks in
 *    cool weather (less than 50 degrees F)". DO NOT use Penn State's
 *    month-precise dates — they are north-eastern. NOT VERIFIED for Texas.
 *  - MIGRATORY BIRDS: 50 CFR 21.14 authorises "The prompt removal of migratory
 *    birds, including nests, eggs, and nestlings, from the interior of
 *    buildings" and explicitly "does not apply to birds or nests on building
 *    exteriors."
 *  - RELOCATION: raccoons, foxes, skunks and coyotes may not be transported at
 *    all in Texas under the rabies-control rules (25 TAC ch. 169; Health &
 *    Safety Code ch. 826) — a Class C misdemeanour. Other nuisance fur-bearers
 *    need written TPWD Wildlife Division authorisation PLUS the receiving
 *    landowner's permission. Exempt parties who do transport a rabies-vector
 *    species must release within a ten-mile radius and within the county of
 *    capture. The honest framing for this site is exclusion and on-site
 *    resolution, NOT relocation.
 *
 * ⚠️ THREE HONESTY FLAGS:
 *  1. NO TEXAS SQUIRREL LITTER MONTHS. Not found in any Texas source. The
 *     Mississippi dates (Jan–Mar, Jun–Aug) are a regional analogue and must be
 *     attributed to Mississippi if used at all. This page uses UC IPM's
 *     season-level statement instead and names no months.
 *  2. NO OPOSSUM SOUND PROFILE. Essentially nothing is sourced. ICWDM's "heavy
 *     walking → raccoon or opossum" is the whole of it.
 *  3. ROOF RATS ARE NOT DOCUMENTED IN THIS REGION. Consistent with
 *     /pest-library/roof-rat-and-norway-rat/ — no source places established
 *     roof rats here. The page describes overhead-nesting behaviour without
 *     asserting the species is present, and lets the job evidence decide.
 */
import BaseLayout from '../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import AnswerBox from '../../../components/AnswerBox.astro';
import Crumbs from '../../../components/Crumbs.astro';
import Faq from '../../../components/Faq.astro';
import Cta from '../../../components/Cta.astro';

const crumbs = [
  { name: 'Home', path: '/' },
  { name: 'Services', path: '/services/' },
  { name: 'Rodent control', path: '/services/rodent-control/' },
  { name: 'Noises in the attic', path: '/services/rodent-control/noises-in-the-attic/' },
];

const quickAnswer =
  'Time of day narrows it fast — daytime running is usually squirrels, night-time scratching is usually rodents — but the wildlife damage literature is blunt that noise is not an accurate way to identify a species. Droppings, hole size and an inspection settle it. And what you are allowed to do next depends on the animal: bats and most birds are protected, and several Texas species cannot legally be relocated at all.';

const faqs = [
