  },
  {
    q: 'How do I tell the droppings apart?',
    a: 'Bat droppings are the distinctive ones. They are segmented, elongated and friable, and when crushed they go powdery and reveal shiny bits of undigested insect remains — because bats eat insects and the wing cases catch the light. Rodent droppings are harder and more fibrous, and they do not crumble the same way. Age matters too: fresh droppings are black, shiny and moist, while old ones are dry and brown or grey. A pile of dry grey droppings and no fresh ones means a problem that has already ended, which is a different job.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How big a hole does each animal need?',
    a: 'Small enough to be discouraging. Fox and grey squirrels use holes about the size of a baseball, red squirrels about the size of a golf ball, and flying squirrels a hole the size of a quarter. AgriLife notes that a half-inch gap is enough for a squirrel to get started, and that rats can get through a gap under a door as narrow as half an inch or a hole the size of a quarter. The usual entry points it names are gaps around the garage door, venting and flashing on the roof, and ground-level gaps under and around siding.',
  },
  {
    q: 'If it is bats, what are the rules?',
