  },
  {
    q: 'When can bats be excluded?',
    a: 'AgriLife gives the Texas window as generally between September and April, depending on where you are in the state, and the reason for that window is stark: evicting the adults during maternity season will orphan and kill the babies inside the building. It also warns the other way — removing the one-way devices too early can permanently seal bats inside, which kills them and creates odour and sanitation problems. Its guidance is to leave the devices in place at least a week in warm weather and two weeks when it is under fifty degrees.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Will you catch it and release it somewhere else?',
    a: 'Usually not, and that is the law rather than a preference. In Texas, raccoons, foxes, skunks and coyotes may not be transported at all under the rabies control rules — doing so is a Class C misdemeanour. Other nuisance fur-bearers can only be relocated with written authorisation from Texas Parks and Wildlife plus the permission of the landowner receiving them. Even parties who are exempt and do move a rabies-vector species must release within a ten-mile radius and inside the county of capture. Our work is exclusion and resolving it on site, and any company promising to take your raccoon out to the country is describing something it should not be doing.',
  },
