<BaseLayout
  title="Noises in the Attic at Night | Trusted Termite"
  description="Daytime or night-time narrows it, but sound alone does not identify an animal. Droppings, hole size, the Texas rules on bats and birds, and why sealing first fails."
  path="/services/rodent-control/noises-in-the-attic/"
  quickAnswer={quickAnswer}
  breadcrumbs={crumbs}
  faqs={faqs}
>
  <Crumbs items={crumbs} />

  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">Rodent control · problem page</p>
      <h1>Something is moving in the attic</h1>
      <div class="measure"><AnswerBox text={quickAnswer} /></div>
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          Almost every page you will find on this subject offers a confident translation table: this sound
          means that animal. We are going to start by undercutting that, because the wildlife damage
          literature does it first. The Internet Center for Wildlife Damage Management — a university
          consortium, not a company — states plainly that noises and sounds in wildlife damage settings are
          not a very accurate way to determine the species causing a problem.
        </p>
        <p>
          That does not make the sound useless. Time of day is a genuine filter and it costs nothing.
          But a filter is not an identification, and the difference matters here more than usual, because
          what you are legally allowed to do next depends entirely on which animal it is.
        </p>
        <p>
          A rodent, a squirrel, a bat and a nesting bird are four completely different jobs with four
          different bodies of law behind them. Getting the identification wrong does not just waste money.
          With bats and with migratory birds it can put you on the wrong side of a federal statute.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band tint">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>What the time of day is worth</h2>
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          <strong>Daytime activity points at squirrels.</strong> UC IPM states that tree squirrels are
          active during the day and are often seen in trees, and that they are most active in early morning
          and late afternoon. Georgia's extension service says it even more directly: attic noise during
          the day could be grey squirrels. If you hear rolling, running and something that sounds
          purposeful at nine in the morning, that is the first place to look.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Night-time activity points at rodents.</strong> Rats are mostly active at night. House
          mice are occasionally seen in daylight but are most active after dark. Scratching that starts
          after the house goes quiet and stops before dawn fits that pattern.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Heavy, deliberate movement points at something larger.</strong> Raccoons are nocturnal,
          and the published noise table puts heavy walking with raccoons or opossums. The distinguishing
          impression people report is weight — you are not hearing scurrying, you are hearing something
          moving one limb at a time.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Bats have their own signature.</strong> The sounds come from vocalisations, grooming,
          scratching, crawling and climbing in attics, under eaves, behind walls and between floors, and
          activity peaks in three specific windows: on hot days in the attic, just before leaving the roost
          at dusk, and on returning at dawn. Noise that clusters tightly at dusk and dawn rather than
          running all night is a real clue.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>And then the caveat, restated because it matters.</strong> The published noise-to-species
          table gives three buckets — scratching in a wall means rats, mice or birds; running in an attic
          means squirrels; heavy walking means a raccoon or an opossum. That is a place to start. Anybody
          who tells you over the phone exactly what animal you have, from a description of a sound, is
          guessing with more confidence than the literature supports.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>The evidence that actually settles it</h2>
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          <strong>Droppings, and specifically whether they crumble.</strong> Bat droppings are segmented,
          elongated and friable — crush one and it goes to powder, showing shiny bits of undigested insect
          remains, because a bat's diet is insects and the hard wing cases survive. Rodent droppings are
          harder and more fibrous and do not do that. This one test separates the most legally complicated
          animal from the most common one, and it takes about four seconds.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Fresh or old.</strong> Georgia's guidance gives the ageing test: fresh droppings are
          black, shiny and moist, while old droppings are dry and brown or grey. A loft full of dry grey
          droppings and nothing fresh is the aftermath of a problem rather than a live one, and the correct
          job there is cleanup and sealing rather than trapping.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>The size of the hole.</strong> This is a better discriminator than most people realise.
          Fox and grey squirrels use holes the size of a baseball. Red squirrels use holes the size of a
          golf ball. Flying squirrels get through a hole the size of a quarter. AgriLife notes a
          half-inch gap is enough for a squirrel to work with, and that rats need only a gap under a door
          as narrow as half an inch or a hole the size of a quarter.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Where the holes usually are, on the houses around here.</strong> AgriLife names gaps
          around the garage door, venting and flashing on the roof, and ground-level gaps under and around
          siding. On the housing stock in Seminole, Andrews, Denver City and the Basin towns, add the
          places wind and sun work on: soffit and fascia joints that have opened up, ridge and turbine vent
          flashing, gable vent screens that have rusted through, and the gap where an evaporative cooler or
          a line set passes through the wall.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Damage tells you too.</strong> AgriLife describes squirrel damage as chewing that
          sometimes opens large holes in soffits and fascia, and chewing on wires. Rodents leave grease
          marks along runs and gnaw damage at gaps. Insulation compressed into tunnels, and a musky odour —
          house mice are noted for a characteristic musky smell — both point at rodents.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band tint">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>The law, which is where most pest websites publish something false</h2>
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          <strong>Bats are protected under the Parks and Wildlife Code, not the health code.</strong> The
          provision is Texas Parks and Wildlife Code section 63.101. It makes it unlawful to hunt a bat, or
          to sell, offer for sale, purchase, offer to purchase, or possess after purchase a bat or any part
          of one, dead or alive. It then permits bats to be removed or hunted inside or on buildings
          occupied by people, and it exempts a defined list including animal control officers, peace
          officers, health officials, anyone transporting a bat for rabies testing, and licensed pest
          control service providers.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>And no chemical, ever.</strong> AgriLife states it flatly: it is a federal violation to
          use chemicals — including insecticides, rodenticides, disinfectants and mothballs — to kill bats.
          Mothballs in an attic against bats is a thing people still do out here, and it is both illegal
          and useless.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Timing on a bat exclusion.</strong> AgriLife gives the eviction window as generally
          between September and April, depending on your location in the state, with maternity season
          running from spring to late summer. Its reasoning is not sentimental: evicting the adults during
          maternity season will orphan and kill the babies inside the building — and then you have dead
          bats in a ceiling void. It also warns in the other direction, that removing one-way devices too
          early can permanently seal bats inside, killing them and causing odour and sanitation problems.
          Its stated durations are at least one week in warm weather and two weeks in cool weather, under
          fifty degrees.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Birds.</strong> Nearly all native birds are protected under the Migratory Bird Treaty
          Act. Federal regulation authorises the prompt removal of migratory birds, including nests, eggs
          and nestlings, from the interior of buildings — and states explicitly that this does not apply to
          birds or nests on building exteriors. So a bird in your attic and a bird nesting in your eave are
          different legal situations. House sparrows, European starlings and pigeons are not protected.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Relocation, which is the promise to be suspicious of.</strong> In Texas, raccoons, foxes,
          skunks and coyotes may not be transported at all under the rabies control rules, and doing so is
          a Class C misdemeanour. Other nuisance fur-bearers may be relocated only with written
          authorisation from the Texas Parks and Wildlife Department's Wildlife Division and the permission
          of the landowner receiving the animal. Even the exempt parties who may transport a rabies-vector
          species have to release it within a ten-mile radius and within the county of capture.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Which is why our answer is exclusion.</strong> Get the animal out, confirm it is out,
          seal the building so it cannot come back. That is legal everywhere, it is the approach every
          extension source recommends first, and it is the only one that fixes the problem rather than
          moving it. If somebody offers to drive your raccoon into the country, they are describing
          something Texas law does not permit.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>Get it out, confirm it, then seal — in that order</h2>
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          <strong>Every source puts the order the same way.</strong> Georgia's guidance is that you must
          locate and seal all entry points and that both must be done to correct the problem. Its published
          hierarchy is habitat modification first, then exclusion, then removal or repellents, and lethal
          control last.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>The confirmation step is the one people skip.</strong> UC IPM gives a test that costs
          nothing and is difficult to argue with: plug the entrance with a loose wad of newspaper, and if
          anything is still inside it will remove the plug to get out. Check it over a couple of days
          before you close anything permanently.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>What sealing an animal in actually costs you.</strong> The animal dies in the void. You
          then have an odour problem for an unpredictable period, secondary insects feeding on the carcass,
          and often a second hole where a trapped animal chewed its way out through something softer. It
          converts a straightforward exclusion into two jobs and a wall repair.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>One-way devices are the tool for this.</strong> AgriLife's recommendation for squirrels
          is installing a one-way door or a repeater trap on the roof. The same principle drives bat
          exclusion, with the durations noted above. The animal leaves under its own power to feed and
          cannot get back in, which is why the device has to stay long enough for every individual to have
          made that trip.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Then the sealing has to be real.</strong> Sheet metal, hardware cloth and proper closure
          at soffit, fascia, vent and flashing. Expanding foam alone is not a rodent barrier and a squirrel
          will chew through it in an afternoon. On the houses here, the recurring failures are the same few
          places: the soffit return at a roof-wall junction, gable vent screen, garage door corner seals,
          and the penetration where a line set or cooler duct goes through.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>Season, and why the same house is quiet for months</h2>
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          <strong>Attic noise is rarely random and it is often annual.</strong> Two things drive it here:
          weather pushing animals toward shelter, and breeding pushing them toward a safe, dry, defensible
          space. An attic is the best of both, and on the Llano Estacado it is often the only one for a
          long way in any direction.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Squirrels breed in the late winter or in the spring,</strong> and depending on the
          species produce one or two litters a year. We are deliberately not printing month-by-month dates,
          because we could not find a Texas source that gives them and we are not going to borrow another
          state's calendar and present it as local. What the season-level statement supports is the pattern
          people report: a house that is quiet for most of the year and loud for a stretch, then quiet
          again once the young disperse.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Raccoons follow a tighter calendar.</strong> Most litters are born in April or May,
          with some late-breeding females — typically those that lost a first litter — not giving birth
          until June, July or August, and a litter runs three to five young. That late tail is why a
          raccoon problem in an attic in July is entirely plausible rather than out of season.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Heat drives its own pattern.</strong> Bat activity in an attic peaks on hot days as well
          as at dusk and dawn — an attic in a Permian Basin August is an extreme environment, and animals
          in it move more, not less. Rodents move the other way in the same weather, coming down out of a
          roasting attic into wall voids and cooler parts of the structure, which is why the noise
          sometimes seems to relocate without anything having changed.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>What the seasonality means practically.</strong> If the noise is new and it is spring,
          you may be listening to something that arrived to give birth, and the job has a timing question
          attached — exclusion carried out while flightless or immobile young are inside creates the exact
          sealed-in problem the last section warned about. If the noise has been there for years and
          returns every autumn, you have a structural gap that nothing has ever closed, and the useful work
          is on the outside of the building rather than in the trap.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band tint">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>What we will and will not tell you on the phone</h2>
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          <strong>We will ask what time of day you hear it,</strong> because that is the single most useful
          thing you can tell us before anybody drives out.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>We will ask what it sounds like</strong> — scratching, running, rolling, gnawing, heavy
          steps, squeaking, chittering — and whether it is one animal or several, and whether it is
          seasonal or new.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>We will ask whether you have been in the attic</strong> and what is on the insulation. If
          you have not, we will not push you up there. It is an unpleasant space, it is a bad place to fall
          through a ceiling, and it is genuinely part of what you are paying us for.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>What we will not do is name the animal down the phone.</strong> The literature does not
          support that level of confidence from a sound, and a wrong identification here is expensive — it
          can mean the wrong tool, the wrong season, or a legally protected animal handled the wrong way.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>And one thing we will say straight.</strong> Rodents are treated on the
          <a href="/services/rodent-control/">rodent control</a> side of this business, and larger animals
          on the <a href="/services/wildlife-removal/">wildlife removal</a> side, with the honesty gates on
          each of those pages. If what you have turns out to be a bat colony in maternity season, the
          correct answer is that we come back in the autumn, and we will tell you that even though it is
          not the answer that gets a job on the books this week.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>Common questions</h2>
      <Faq items={faqs} />
    </div>
  </section>

  <Cta
    heading="Hearing something overhead?"
    body="Tell us the time of day, the kind of sound, and how long it has been going on. Those three answers put us in the right part of the list before we ever open the hatch."
  />
</BaseLayout>
